<%# Icon tile - a glyph on a soft tinted rounded square.

    design.md: `grid place-items-center rounded-xl bg-{semantic}-50 text-{semantic}-600`, for
    contexts where the icon is the *subject* - a KPI figure, a section header, a list-item
    leading icon. Not for a message bar or alert, which gets a plain tone-coloured glyph:
    a -50 tile on a -50 surface measured 1.07:1 and had to shout to be seen.

    This existed longhand in six places with the tone spelled inline, which is the shape
    design.md warns about - a style written in more than one place survives every sweep of
    one of them. The tones are the badge's tones, so a tile and a badge for the same state
    cannot drift apart.

    Two sizes, and the distinction is what the glyph sits next to:

      default - 36px, a 20px glyph. The tile is the subject: a KPI card, an empty state.
      compact - 32px, a 16px glyph. The tile sits on a title's line in a card header, where
                a 36px block would out-height the 16px title it labels.

    A numeral may take the icon's place (`label:`), which is what the getting-started steps use:
    design.md hue-codes the tile and keeps a numeral slate-900, so a step number is this pattern
    with a digit in it rather than a saturated brand disc with white text.

    Locals:
      icon  - Lucide icon name
      label - a short string (a numeral) instead of an icon
      tone  - :neutral (default) :success :warning :danger :info
      compact - true for the 32px header-row size %>
<% tone = local_assigns.fetch(:tone, :neutral).to_sym %>
<% tones = {} %>
<% tones[:neutral] = "bg-slate-100 text-slate-600" %>
<% tones[:success] = "bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-700" %>
<% tones[:warning] = "bg-amber-50 text-amber-700" %>
<% tones[:danger] = "bg-rose-50 text-rose-700" %>
<% tones[:info] = "bg-blue-50 text-blue-700" %>

<% box = local_assigns[:compact] ? "size-8" : "size-9" %>
<% glyph = local_assigns[:compact] ? "size-4" : "size-5" %>

<%# A numeral is content, not decoration, but the ordered list it sits in already conveys the
    order, so it is hidden from assistive tech the way the icons are.

    tag.span, not an interpolated attribute: interpolating an optional attribute yields an
    *unquoted* one, which CSS and Capybara selectors will not match. tag omits nil entirely. %>
<% numeral = local_assigns[:label].present? %>
<%= tag.span class: ["grid", box, "shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-xl",
                     tones.fetch(tone, tones[:neutral]),
                     ("text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 tabular-nums" if numeral)],
             aria: { hidden: (true if numeral) } do %>
  <% if numeral %>
    <%= label %>
  <% else %>
    <%= lucide_icon icon, class: glyph %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
